      "text": "To solve this problem, we will first find the prime factorization of 96 and then determine the prime factorization of 12. After that, we will express 96 as a product of the prime factors of 12, and then determine the number of positive factors of 96 that are also multiples of 12. \n\nLet's start with the prime factorization of 12. We can see that:\n\n\\[12 = 2^2 \\times 3^1\\]\n\nNext, we express 96 with the same prime factors but in a form that will help us count the number of positive factors that are multiples of 12. Notice that 96 can be factored as:\n\n\\[96 = 2^5 \\times 3^1\\]\n\nWe can check if 96 has all the factors of 12 by examining each group of prime factors:\n\n- All of the 2's in 96 must be there to make 96 a multiple of 12 (i.e., 2's in the 30th column count, so 2's in the 29th, 28th, and 1st columns count also) for 12 to be divisible by 12.\n- Similar to 2's, all of the 3's must also be there (i.e., one more times) to make 96 divisible by 12 (i.e., 3's in the 2nd row count for 12 to be divisible by 12).\n\nAfterward, to choose the valid number of \\(n\\) or \\(2^m \\times 3^k \\times \\text{and} \\text{additional primes}\\):\n\n\\[\\text{Factor}^{\\(\\text{n} \\times \\(\\text{number of factors for a given primefactor}\\)}/\\(\\text{primefactor mrscholar}\n)}\\]\n\nLet \\(n = 96\\) and its prime factor be 2. Therefore, the total number of factors is the division of nine factors by one factor for each prime:\n\n\\[\\boxed{9}\\]",
